            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/36/83736/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Zoey YJ Chung",
                "email": "zoey.yj.chung.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "zoeychung10"
            },
            "createdOn": 1758003891,
            "author": {
                "name": "Zoey YJ Chung",
                "email": "zoey.yj.chung.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "zoeychung10"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we have any alternatives here?  How does phosphor-state-manager expect these kinds of scenarios to be handled?\n\nI don't know of any other machine that has a special service for failure on specific boot sequence steps.  It seems like this should be a solved problem elsewhere already?  Maybe check with @geissonator@yahoo.com for additional ideas."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Zoey YJ Chung",
                        "email": "zoey.yj.chung.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "zoeychung10"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @geissonator@yahoo.com May I ask if there is a recommended approach for handling failure scenarios in phosphor-state-manager?\n\nIf there are any relevant documents or examples available, I would greatly appreciate it if you could point me in the right direction."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi, please read over https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/master/architecture/openbmc-systemd.md#error-handling-of-systemd. Basically all PSM systemd targets have OnFailure commands which will start the opposite systemd target, which will cancel all services assuming they have the proper Conflicts statements."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Zoey YJ Chung",
                        "email": "zoey.yj.chung.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "zoeychung10"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the detailed explanation and the link!"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/host-powerreset-failure",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why doesn't this stuff happen automatically when we fail the service?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/host-powerreset-failure",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Zoey YJ Chung",
                        "email": "zoey.yj.chung.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "zoeychung10"
                    },
                    "message": "Based on the provided documentation, I tested using the *RequiredBy* approach, \nwhere the target fails if the service fails. However, since the target failure \noccurs later than the OnFailure handling, the D-Bus status still becomes inconsistent \nby reboot-host service. Therefore, it is still necessary to stop the service promptly \nwithin the script.\n\nI also tested the *OnFailure* approach (OnFailure=obmc-chassis-poweroff@%i.target), \nbut the result was not as expected \u2014 it caused the AC off \u2192 on action to be executed an extra time."
                }
            ],
